course: dictionary
lesson: 5172
title: Isle and island
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An isle is an island, a piece of land with sea all around it. Read the words around it to check. In «The Isle of Man, Jersey and Guernsey are Crown Dependencies», the names of places tell you isle is part of a place name.
explain: Isle is used mostly in poems, songs and place names, which is why it sounds old and special. You will hear it in the Isle of Wight, the British Isles and the Isle of Skye. In ordinary talk, people usually say island instead.
explain: Here is a quick check. Swap island into the sentence in place of isle. If the meaning stays the same, you have found the right meaning. If it does not make sense, read the words around it again.
fact: Isle means an island, especially a small one.
fact: Isle is mostly used in place names and in poems, such as the Isle of Wight.
fact: The British Isles is a group of islands, including Great Britain and Ireland.
fact: Emerald Isle is another name for Ireland.
fact: Island comes from an old word with no s, but in the 1500s people wrongly linked it to isle and added an s that is never spoken.
exercise: «The Isle of Man, Jersey and Guernsey are Crown Dependencies.» What does isle mean in this sentence? | *An island | A river that runs through a town | A road that leads to the sea | A mountain covered in snow
exercise: Which country is called the Emerald Isle? | England | Wales | Scotland | *Ireland
exercise: Which word means the same as isle? | Lake | *Island | Cave | Hill
test: Which is a place name that uses isle? | Lake District | River Thames | Table Mountain | *Isle of Wight
test: Island has an s that is never said. Why is it there? | It shows the island is very big | *It was added in the 1500s when people wrongly linked it to isle | It is the first letter of the sea | It is an old Viking word that was never changed
exam: Which sentence uses isle correctly? | I walked to the isle to buy bread from the shop | The isle was sitting on my chair at lunch | *We sailed across the sea to the isle, where seals rested on the rocks | I drank a glass of isle with my tea
